(# Mission Control Token Spike Postmortem) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Executive Summary) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(This report explains why Mission Control burned through a catastrophic amount ) Tj
T*
(of tokens and thread/session churn during the benchmark effort focused on ) Tj
T*
(project `11` \(`Loop Improvements Shadow Repo R6 Benchmark`\). The short version) Tj
T*
( is brutal: Mission Control kept spawning and re-spawning worker runs faster ) Tj
T*
(than it was closing valid work, it continued operating through model-policy ) Tj
T*
(failures and usage-limit failures, it allowed the same task lanes to be ) Tj
T*
(retried dozens to more than a hundred times, and I did not pull the emergency ) Tj
T*
(brake soon enough.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Local Mission Control telemetry records **715,075,711 tokens** across all ) Tj
T*
(stored runs, including **684,853,363 tokens** for project `11` alone. The ) Tj
T*
(user-supplied Codex profile screenshot reports a **1.5B peak-token day** and ) Tj
T*
(**2,103 total threads**. Those two facts are compatible rather than ) Tj
T*
(contradictory: the local database is clearly undercounting the full Codex-side) Tj
T*
( billing footprint, while the local run volume and thread-spawn patterns ) Tj
T*
(explain how the profile number could reach that scale.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Most importantly, this was not one bug. It was a stacked failure chain: weak ) Tj
T*
(run-budget controls, missing circuit breakers, retry churn, scheduler ) Tj
T*
(supersession churn, policy-invalid model assignments, usage-limit handling ) Tj
T*
(that did not force a clean stop, and bad operator supervision from me.) Tj
T*
() Tj
