(## NTSB-Style Breakdown) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Factual Sequence) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. A live benchmark orchestration was launched with a swarm-oriented plan and ) Tj
T*
(aggressive throughput goals.) Tj
T*
(2. Mission Control generated many parallel defect-batch lanes and began ) Tj
T*
(creating worker runs at scale.) Tj
T*
(3. Some lanes completed real work, but the system also created high-volume ) Tj
T*
(retries, superseded lanes, and review-only churn.) Tj
T*
(4. Invalid model-policy selections triggered runner-bug failures instead of a ) Tj
T*
(global stop.) Tj
T*
(5. Usage-limit failures appeared in stored run reports, but the system did not) Tj
T*
( enter a durable kill state.) Tj
T*
(6. More unblock/re-run/superseded tasks were generated, amplifying thread ) Tj
T*
(count and token usage.) Tj
T*
(7. I continued babysitting and recovery attempts instead of terminating the ) Tj
T*
(benchmark as soon as it became economically irrational.) Tj
T*
(8. The user profile eventually showed a 1.5B peak-token day and 2,103 total ) Tj
T*
(threads.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Probable Cause) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The probable cause was uncontrolled run amplification inside Mission Control: ) Tj
T*
(the orchestrator lacked hard economic guardrails and allowed the same logical ) Tj
T*
(work to be executed repeatedly across fresh worker sessions, even after ) Tj
T*
(invalid-model errors, usage-limit signals, no-change outcomes, and scheduler ) Tj
T*
(supersession churn indicated the benchmark was no longer behaving like a ) Tj
T*
(disciplined queue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Contributing Factors) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. No global token budget or cost circuit breaker.) Tj
